course: dictionary
lesson: 2747
title: A disagreement is not always a fight
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A disagreement is when people have different opinions about something. It can be small, like a quarrel about which film to watch, or it can be about a bigger problem. You can spot the word's clues in the sentences around it. «Arguing over paint colours like "Magnolia" or "Duck Egg Blue" is a small disagreement about taste, not a real fight, and it ends with a choice or a compromise.» The sentence tells you what a disagreement is: people want different colours, and they settle it by choosing one or by meeting in the middle.
explain: The sentence also tells you that a disagreement is not the same as a fight. «Hitting, kicking or grabbing never solves a disagreement fairly.» So a disagreement is about different ideas, and a fight uses hands and feet. Disagreeing politely is fine.
explain: A disagreement does not have to end a friendship. «Disagreement does not have to end a friendship.» Good friends can think differently and still like each other. To settle a disagreement, you can listen, take turns, explain your view, find a compromise, or ask an adult to help.
explain: Words in the same family help too. Disagree is the verb, and disagreements is the plural. You can also say you are in disagreement with someone, or that you have a disagreement about or over something. If you know that disagree means not to think the same, you can work out disagreement without looking it up.
fact: Disagreement is a noun. Its plural is disagreements.
fact: «Disagreement» means when people have different opinions about something, or a small argument or quarrel.
fact: Disagreement can also mean not matching, as in a disagreement between two answers.
fact: A «tribunal» is a kind of court that settles disagreements.
fact: Useful phrases: have a disagreement, in disagreement, a disagreement about or over something, settle a disagreement.
exercise: «Arguing over paint colours like "Magnolia" or "Duck Egg Blue" is a small disagreement about taste, not a real fight.» What is a disagreement in this sentence? | A list of colours for a picture | *A small argument about different opinions | A shop sale on paint | A hard fight with hitting and kicking
exercise: «Disagreement does not have to end a friendship.» What does this sentence tell us? | Friends must never disagree | Friendship is only for grown-ups | A disagreement always ends a friendship | *Friends can disagree and still be friends
exercise: Which is the fairest way to settle a disagreement? | Hitting or grabbing the toy | Shouting until the other person gives up | *Listen, take turns and find a compromise | Walking off and never speaking again
test: «Hitting, kicking or grabbing never solves a disagreement fairly.» What does this sentence teach us? | *Violence is not a fair way to settle a disagreement | Grabbing is a kind of game | Disagreements are always solved by kicking | Hitting is a fair way to win
test: Which word means nearly the same as a small quarrel? | *Disagreement | Agreement | Surprise | Compromise
exam: Which sentence uses disagreement correctly? | She disagreement the cake on Tuesday. | *The two answers were in disagreement, so I checked my working. | We went to the park and had a big disagreement of sandwiches. | Disagreement is a sleepy dog that barks at night.
